src/test/stress/README

Stress tests for CONCURRENTLY commands
======================================

This directory stresses the commands that are meant to behave
transparently to other sessions: the CONCURRENTLY variants of REPACK,
CREATE INDEX, REINDEX, DROP INDEX, REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW and ALTER
TABLE ... DETACH PARTITION, together with the machinery they lean on --
logical decoding, replication slots, the visibility map, the plan cache.

Each test runs a pgbench workload whose clients maintain an invariant
while one of those commands runs against the same tables, and checks
that invariant along with the resulting indexes and every server log.
Any SQL error or broken invariant makes pgbench abort
(--exit-on-abort), failing the test.  The tests are not deterministic:
they rely on timing and randomized workloads, so a passing run does not
prove the absence of bugs.  They are cheap enough to run by default,
and can be scaled up.

How a test is built
===================

A test file under t/ is a scenario: which loads run, which commands
rotate against them, and against what cluster.  Everything else is
composed.  The pieces live in bundle files under t/Stress/:

  Feature/*.pm   one file per dimension: its schema decorator, the
                 loads that drive it, the checks that hold it to its
                 invariant, and the DDL aimed at it -- everything about
                 the ledger is in Ledger.pm and nowhere else
  Cross/*.pm     what cuts across features: the shared DDL rotation,
                 the generic checks, the topologies, disruptors and
                 settings profiles, the modifiers, the chaos machinery

A bundle declares entries into the registries (see Stress::Registry);
a scenario names them, and Stress::Compose resolves what it wrote into
what runs:

  - the schemas the named loads, commands, indexes and checks require
    are pulled in, transitively, in dependency order;
  - each load and each command brings the checks that verify it
    (tpcb_like carries balances, toggle_data_checksums carries
    no_checksum_failures), and generic checks -- amcheck, no_slot_leak,
    the log scan, and the gated ones like index_vs_seq -- join wherever
    their requirements are already satisfied;
  - a check that arrived implicitly and conflicts with a load present
    is dropped with a note; no_checks => [...] takes named ones back
    out, loudly if they were never in.

Every run notes the effective composition with the provenance of each
piece:

  effective repack_dml_s1: schema=[pgbench] checks=[balances<load:tpcb_like,
    amcheck<auto,...] topology=standalone mvcc_gap=tolerated

so what a run verified is in its log rather than in anyone's
reconstruction.  The cluster is described on three axes, up to one of
each: a topology (standalone, standby, subscription -- how many nodes
and how they relate), a disruptor (crash_loop, cancellation -- what
happens to the cluster while the workload runs), and a settings profile
(wal_replica, autovacuum, lock_exhaustion -- a cluster configured to
work differently).  The axes carry their own requires/conflicts and
compose; scenarios that differ only in an axis are declared once as a
scenario_template in their bundle, with a five-line stub file per
variant.

t/001_meta.pl is the framework checking itself with almost no cluster:
the registries' cross-references, every scenario's resolution, the
composition rules, the log scanner's verdicts on synthetic logs, and
the chaos catalogue against the build (below).  When changing the
framework, run it first; it fails in seconds with the declaration site
in the message.

Duration scaling and reproducing a failure
==========================================

The run time of each test is controlled by the stress_concurrently value
in PG_TEST_EXTRA:

    meson test --suite stress    # after PG_TEST_EXTRA='stress_concurrently=10'

Each scenario is calibrated so that a value of 1 makes it run for about
six seconds, and the increase should be roughly linear; a value of 0
disables the suite.  If PG_TEST_EXTRA does not mention
stress_concurrently at all, a value of 1 is assumed, except in
environments that declare themselves slow by setting
PG_TEST_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT (such as Valgrind buildfarm animals), where the
suite is skipped.

Every run reports the seed it used:

    # stress_seed is 1336750692

Passing it back reproduces the same choices, both in Perl and in
pgbench:

    PG_TEST_EXTRA='stress_seed=1336750692'

Soak mode (see t/Stress/Soak.pm) numbers every combination it runs --
only those, since combinations whose pieces do not fit are discarded
before they are numbered -- and which combination a number names depends
only on the seed, so a failure reported as invented_57 comes back
without repeating the hour before it:

    PG_TEST_EXTRA='stress_mode=soak stress_seed=1336750692
                   stress_soak_skip=57 stress_soak_count=1'

The same two settings split a long soak into shorter runs that add up to
it; each run reports the index to resume from.

The generated pgbench scripts are written into the test's data directory
and echoed into its log, and the pgbench command line is logged too, so
a failing scenario can be replayed by hand.  Every node also runs with
verbose errors, backtraces for relation_open, log_lock_waits, and the
SQLSTATE in its log_line_prefix: these produce no output unless
something goes wrong, and a failure that arrives without its call site
is expensive to chase.

Chaos
=====

Most of the windows these tests hunt are microseconds wide.  Repetition
does not find those: a scenario can run for hours without ever placing
two operations within a microsecond of each other.  What finds them is
widening the window, for a small fraction of the operations that reach
it, until it is wide enough to lose.

A chaos profile does that.  It attaches jitter -- a sleep of a given
length, taken with a given probability -- to injection points in the
paths these races live in, and can turn on the probabilistic form of
debug_discard_caches for the cache-flush hazards.  The named profiles
are in t/Stress/Cross/Chaos.pm (a feature bundle may declare its own,
as DataChecksums.pm does), and a scenario asks for one with

  chaos => 'light',

which is orthogonal to the axes.

The pool an invented profile draws from is not a hand-written list.
The build scans the backend sources for injection point call sites
(injection_points_defined(), see the injection_points module) and the
pool is that catalogue minus what must not be jittered: the
IS_INJECTION_POINT_ATTACHED sites, where attachment alone changes what
the server decides, are excluded mechanically, and %CHAOS_EXCLUDED
holds any judged exclusions beyond that.  A point somebody has reasoned
about carries curated caps in %CHAOS_POINTS -- how likely and how long
its sleep may be, and those caps are not decoration: a point every
commit reaches has to stay rare, a point in a lock queue has to stay
short.  Everything else gets conservative defaults (at most 1% of
passes, at most 5ms).  So a point added anywhere in the tree joins the
pool on the next build, at caps too small to break a run and large
enough to widen a window; 001_meta notes the uncurated names, and fails
on a curated or excluded name the tree no longer defines -- attaching
to a stale name is silent by design, so this is the only place the rot
can be caught.

Soak does not pick from the named profiles.  It invents one -- a few
points from the pool, each drawn inside its caps -- because there are
far more combinations of points than anyone would write down and the
interesting ones are pairs nobody thought to pair.  Most combinations
get none, so that a failure under chaos can still be told from one that
does not need it.  The invented profile is printed with the rest of the
combination and comes back with the seed.

Two properties are what make a chaos failure worth reporting, and both
are deliberate.  A profile only ever delays: nothing the server decides
changes because of it, so a failure seen under chaos is a failure that
exists without it.  And no sleep approaches lock_timeout -- a lock
request that dawdles at the head of a queue stalls every writer behind
it, which ends a run in a cascade that tested nothing.

Every run reports what the jitter did, per point:

  # chaos 'decoding': 186 sleeps totalling 7514092us
  # chaos 'decoding': commit-before-clog-update: 186 sleeps totalling 7514092us

with a diagnostic for an attached point that never fired, because a
profile of five points that fired four times zero is testing one point
while claiming five, and the aggregate alone cannot tell those apart.

A build without injection points ignores all of it, with a note.

Modifiers and the settings pool
===============================

A modifier is a named set of GUCs at values that change how the server
does its work without changing what the work produces: everything
spills instead of fitting in memory, the planner picks a different
node, WAL is really flushed to disk, a vacuum freezes at once.  They
are in t/Stress/Cross/Modifiers.pm, a scenario asks for one with
modifier => 'spill', and soak gives one to every combination.  The
rules -- outcome-neutral, never disabling what a scenario exists to
test, every applied value read back and reported -- are documented
with the registry.

The settings pool (t/Stress/Cross/SettingsPool.pm) is the uncurated
kind of the same variation: one knob at a time, at the ends of its
range, drawn a few per combination by soak so that pairs nobody would
write down get tried anyway.  The modifier rules bind here too, and a
knob the chosen modifier or profile already sets is ineligible for
that combination rather than silently overridden.

The REPACK tolerance
====================

REPACK (CONCURRENTLY) is not MVCC-safe yet: a snapshot spanning its
relfilenode swap may see a table empty.  Which checks must allow for
that is derived, not configured: a %DDL entry that is not MVCC-safe
carries mvcc_safe => 0, and only a scenario whose rotation carries such
a command gets the tolerant form of the checks -- one that never
repacks is strict without asking.  stress_strict_mvcc=1 in
PG_TEST_EXTRA forces the strict form everywhere, which is how to find
out whether REPACK has become MVCC-safe; when it has, deleting the
mvcc_safe keys is the whole change.  See Stress::MVCC.

The log scan
============

After every node has stopped, the framework reads each server log whole
and fails the run on the unambiguous evidence of a server-side defect:
any PANIC, an assertion trap, a segfault, a FATAL beyond the
allowlisted lifecycle chatter, and any ERROR or WARNING whose SQLSTATE
is internal_error or a corruption class.  This is what catches the
failures no client saw -- an autovacuum worker's crash, a checksum
worker's assertion -- which used to pass silently.  Allowlist entries
are declared next to the thing that earns the noise (the standby
topology allows its recovery-conflict disconnects; the crash loop
allows what an immediate shutdown says), plain ERRORs are deliberately
out of scope, and PANIC cannot be allowlisted at all.
stress_log_scan=warn downgrades findings during bring-up; =off disables
the scan.  See Stress::LogScan.

Reproducers for open bugs
=========================

A scenario tagged 'open-bug' reproduces a bug that has no fix yet, so it
fails on purpose.  Those are skipped unless

  PG_TEST_EXTRA='stress_open_bugs=1'

which keeps the suite green for the failures nobody expects, and keeps
soak from walking into one.  The tag comes off when the fix lands, at
which point the scenario becomes an ordinary guard against reverting it.

Replaying a catch
=================

REGRESSIONS records, for each historical bug, whether reverting its fix
has been shown to make this suite fail.  The commits at the end of this
branch make those claims executable: each is a pair --

  Revert "<fix>" to expose the bug
  Reapply "<fix>"

-- so that checking out the first of a pair gives a tree where the named
scenario fails, and the second puts it back.  The tip is unaffected: the
two commits are exact inverses, which is worth checking with

  git diff <reapply-commit>~2 <reapply-commit>

which must be empty.

That is what makes them useful after the framework is refactored.  A
scenario that still passes proves nothing on its own -- it may have
stopped testing anything.  To confirm it still catches what it used to:

  git cherry-pick -n <the revert commit of the pair>
  ninja -C buildDir && meson test -C buildDir --suite setup
  meson test -C buildDir --suite stress <scenario>

and it must fail, with the message the revert commit's own message
quotes; then git reset --hard and rebuild.  Rebuilding after the reset
is not optional: a tmp_install left holding the reverted binaries makes
every later run test the bug instead of the tree, which has burned this
suite once already.  Each revert commit names its scenario, the failure
rate that was measured, and the symptom, so there is nothing to look
up.

Several of the reverts could not be produced with "git revert" -- the
surrounding code has moved since the fix -- and were written by hand.
Those commits say so and say what the hand-written change amounts to,
because a hand-written revert that is subtly wrong produces a failure
that means nothing, which happened more than once while this suite was
being built.

Running the tests
=================

NOTE: You must have given the --enable-tap-tests argument to configure,
or built with meson (injection points enabled if chaos should do
anything: -Dinjection_points=true).

Run
    make check
or
    make installcheck
or, under meson,
    meson test --suite stress

See src/test/perl/README for more info about running these tests.
